  "marks": "Mastering: The response analyses what the shared sonnet form is designed to communicate to the audience at this moment (a sense of perfect union, of two people already completing each other rhythmically before they complete each other narratively). The student explains both a performer choice (e.g. Romeo's vocal pace tightening to match Juliet's answering rhythm as the exchange progresses; a dropped gaze on the first kiss that lands on the couplet's final beat) and a design choice (e.g. a lighting special narrowing around the pair, the feast soundscape fading beneath them) with a clear effect on the audience named (wonder, inevitability, a tightening of focus). A substantiated judgement is offered about why this formal choice is dramatically more powerful than a simple exchange.\n\nSecure: The student identifies the sonnet structure and links it to a sense of romantic union, names at least one performer choice and one design choice anchored to the scene, with a generally accurate effect on the audience.\n\nDeveloping: The student identifies the sonnet and says it shows love, but the analysis is general and no concrete staging choices are named; effect on audience is implied rather than stated.\n\nEmerging: The student identifies the scene and makes basic observations about love or poetry without linking to performance or design."
