+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n18\">The Nurse and Friar Lawrence are the play&rsquo;s surrogate adults &mdash; closer to the lovers than their actual parents, and therefore more culpable when things go wrong. Shakespeare gives the Nurse almost all her dialogue in prose, which is the language of servants and comic characters in the play. The exception is Act Four, when she believes Juliet is dead: she shifts into verse. A performer&rsquo;s awareness of that verse choice as a character signal &mdash; even prose-speakers resort to the formal language of grief &mdash; is the kind of dramatic analysis examiners reward.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n18\">The Nurse and Friar Lawrence are the play&rsquo;s surrogate adults &mdash; closer to the lovers than their actual parents, and therefore more culpable when things go wrong. The Nurse uses both prose and verse. Prose often suits her comic, conversational exchanges, while her verse in the discovery scene can sharpen the force of her grief. A performer can use these shifts in form as one clue to changes in pace and emotional pressure.</p>",
