+    "lesson": 5,
+    "field": "conclusion_html",
+    "find": "<li data-narration-id=\"n22\">Prose versus verse signals social status and emotional register &mdash; the Nurse&rsquo;s shift into verse in Act 4 is a performance cue, not just a literary fact, and a good answer translates every formal choice into specific vocal, physical and design decisions.</li>",
+    "replace": "<li data-narration-id=\"n22\">Prose and verse can signal social status and emotional register &mdash; the Nurse uses both forms, and her verse in Act Four can guide specific vocal and physical choices.</li>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F8: The conclusion repeated the false claim of a unique Act Four shift."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 5,
+    "field": "practice_questions",
+    "path": "[3].marks",
+    "expect": "1 mark for naming a lighting choice for verse scenes (e.g. warm amber wash for the feast); 1 mark for naming a contrasting lighting choice for comic prose scenes (e.g. brighter, more even state for the Nurse's scenes); 1 mark for explaining the effect of each on how the audience reads the character's status or mood; 1 mark for referencing a specific named moment (e.g. the Nurse's shift to verse in Act 4 and a corresponding design shift to cooler pale blue).",
+    "value": "1 mark for naming a lighting choice for verse scenes (e.g. warm amber wash for the feast); 1 mark for naming a contrasting lighting choice for comic prose scenes (e.g. brighter, more even state for the Nurse's scenes); 1 mark for explaining the effect of each on how the audience reads the character's status or mood; 1 mark for referencing a specific named moment (e.g. the Nurse's verse in Act 4 and a corresponding design shift to cooler pale blue).",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
