+  {
+    "lesson": 8,
+    "field": "content_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n17\">Exam answers on practitioners fail when they describe the practitioner&rsquo;s biography without applying the technique to a specific moment of the play. The question is always: what would a director drawing on this practitioner DO differently at this named moment? A good answer names the technique, explains what the director would do and names the effect on the audience &mdash; all three, in sequence.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n17\">When using a practitioner in an exam answer, apply the technique to a specific moment of the play. Name the technique, explain the practical choice it informs and state the effect on the audience.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F16: AQA does not set a distinct practitioner question in its set-text question sequence."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 8,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n18\">Practitioner questions are where students most often describe a biography instead of making an application. Writing &ldquo;Stanislavski was a Russian theatre director who founded the Moscow Art Theatre&rdquo; contributes nothing to a Drama answer. What matters is: if a director applies Stanislavski&rsquo;s objective method to Juliet in the tomb scene, what does the performer DO differently? What vocal and physical choices follow from having a precise, internally motivated objective? For the 20-mark extended staging response, show you know more than one approach: compare what a Stanislavskian production would do for a named moment with what a physical theatre or Brechtian production would do &mdash; and make a directorial decision, justifying your choice. That comparative judgement is what separates the top band from the second band.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n18\">When a practitioner informs a set-text answer, apply the technique to a named moment rather than reciting a biography. For the 20-mark response, develop either the named performer&rsquo;s role or one selected design skill, and justify choices for the extract and the play as a whole.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F16: AQA's 20-mark set-text task does not require a comparison of practitioners and is answered in one role."
