+    "lesson": 5,
+    "field": "content_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n11\">Shakespeare does not give every character verse. The Nurse, the servants and Peter speak almost entirely in <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"Speech in plays that is written without metrical structure, typically used for lower-status characters or comic exchanges.\">prose</dfn>. This is not random: prose signals lower social status, earthiness and comedy. A performer playing the Nurse can find permission in this: the Nurse does not have to modulate her delivery to a regular beat. She can interrupt herself, trail off, repeat, speed up in excitement, slow down in self-pity &mdash; the prose form invites natural, <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"Depicting life as it is, without idealisation, with authentic speech, behaviour and psychology.\">naturalistic</dfn> vocal behaviour in a way that strict verse does not.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n11\">Shakespeare does not give every character verse. The Nurse uses both prose and verse. Prose often suits her comic, conversational exchanges, while verse can sharpen emotional intensity. A performer can use the form as one clue to changes in pace, breath and pressure.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F8: The Nurse does not speak almost entirely in prose."
