+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n22\">On playwright&rsquo;s intentions questions about themes, the trap is to describe a theme and then list evidence without explaining what Shakespeare wants the audience to <em>feel</em> or <em>understand</em> at that specific moment. The command word &ldquo;analyse&rdquo; means: identify the technique, explain how it works, state its effect on the audience, and make a judgement about why Shakespeare made that choice. For <em>Romeo and Juliet</em>, examiners note that the best answers hold fate and free will in tension rather than choosing one: the play seems to offer both simultaneously, and that is the point. In design and performer questions about themes, be concrete &mdash; a gobo of star patterns on the stage floor, a colour-coded costume scheme, a performer&rsquo;s movement vocabulary that slows with age. &ldquo;Dark lighting to show the theme of fate&rdquo; is the response that will not progress beyond the developing band.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n22\">For the 12-mark set-text question, focus on the named performer&rsquo;s use of performance space and interaction in the extract. Explain specific choices and their effect on the audience; do not turn the response into a literary essay about theme alone.</p>",
