+    "lesson": 5,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n18\">For the 12-mark question asking you to analyse the playwright&rsquo;s intentions, students frequently make the mistake of writing a literary essay: &ldquo;Shakespeare uses iambic pentameter to convey Romeo&rsquo;s nobility.&rdquo; This will not access the top band. You must link the form to a specific staging choice and a specific audience effect: what does a director DO with the verse form, and what does the audience FEEL as a result? Aim to discuss both a performer&rsquo;s vocal response to the verse AND a design choice that reinforces the form at the same named moment. For the 20-mark extended staging response, examiners want breadth: performer choices AND lighting AND sound AND costume, all anchored to the same moment of the play &mdash; never drift into generalities about the whole play.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n18\">For the 12-mark set-text question, explain how the named performer would use performance space and interact with the other actor in the shaded extract. Use verse only where it informs a specific vocal, physical or spatial choice.</p>",
