+    "lesson": 1,
+    "field": "content_html",
+    "find": "Romeo learns of Juliet&rsquo;s apparent death, races to Verona, kills Paris at the tomb, and poisons himself beside Juliet&rsquo;s body just as she wakes.",
+    "replace": "Romeo learns of Juliet&rsquo;s apparent death, races to Verona, kills Paris at the tomb, and poisons himself beside Juliet&rsquo;s body. Juliet wakes shortly afterwards.",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F1: Romeo dies before Juliet wakes."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 1,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n20\">On extended staging questions about structure and pace, the most common mistake is to describe <em>what happens</em> act by act instead of explaining <em>how a director communicates the structural shift to an audience</em>. For a question about the play&rsquo;s turning point, for example, you should identify Act Three as the pivot and then immediately move to concrete design and performer choices that signal the change: a lighting palette shift from warm ambers to cold blue-whites, a performer&rsquo;s physical energy becoming jagged rather than fluid, a soundscape losing its musical underscore and replacing it with silence. Every structural observation needs a staging consequence. Also remember the prologue: students who mention the chorus&rsquo;s role in establishing dramatic irony from the opening moments tend to score more highly on playwright&rsquo;s intentions questions, because they show awareness that the audience&rsquo;s knowledge shapes how every scene lands.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n20\">On the 20-mark set-text question, follow the role in the question rather than retelling the plot. As a performer, turn structural ideas into vocal, physical and spatial choices. As a designer, develop one design skill and explain how it supports the action in the extract and the play as a whole.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F2: AQA's 20-mark set-text task is answered as either a performer or a designer, not as a combined staging response."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 2,
+    "field": "content_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n18\">The Nurse and Friar Lawrence are the play&rsquo;s surrogate adults &mdash; closer to the lovers than their actual parents, and therefore more culpable when things go wrong. Shakespeare gives the Nurse almost all her dialogue in prose, which is the language of servants and comic characters in the play. The exception is Act Four, when she believes Juliet is dead: she shifts into verse. A performer&rsquo;s awareness of that verse choice as a character signal &mdash; even prose-speakers resort to the formal language of grief &mdash; is the kind of dramatic analysis examiners reward.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n18\">The Nurse and Friar Lawrence are the play&rsquo;s surrogate adults &mdash; closer to the lovers than their actual parents, and therefore more culpable when things go wrong. The Nurse uses both prose and verse. Prose often suits her comic, conversational exchanges, while her verse in the discovery scene can sharpen the force of her grief. A performer can use these shifts in form as one clue to changes in pace and emotional pressure.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F3: The Nurse uses verse as well as prose before Act Four."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 2,
+    "field": "conclusion_html",
+    "find": "<li data-narration-id=\"n26\">The Nurse&rsquo;s shift from prose into verse when she believes Juliet is dead is a structural signal that even the most earthy characters resort to formal language under the pressure of grief &mdash; a detail that rewards close reading in performance.</li>",
+    "replace": "<li data-narration-id=\"n26\">The Nurse uses prose and verse; her verse in the discovery scene can sharpen the force of grief and guide a performer&rsquo;s vocal choices.</li>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F3: The conclusion repeated the false claim that Act Four is the Nurse's sole use of verse."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 2,
+    "field": "knowledge_checks",
+    "path": "[1].q",
+    "expect": "Which character speaks almost entirely in prose, with one notable shift into verse in Act Four?",
+    "value": "Which character uses both prose and verse, including verse in Act Four’s discovery scene?",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F3: The question premise incorrectly treats Act Four as the Nurse's only verse."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 2,
+    "field": "flashcard_questions",
+    "path": "[2].a",
+    "expect": "Prose — she only shifts to verse when she believes Juliet is dead.",
+    "value": "She uses both prose and verse; verse in the discovery scene can intensify her grief.",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F3: The answer incorrectly treats the Act Four verse as a unique shift."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 3,
+    "field": "content_html",
+    "find": "The chorus figure exits after the prologue and does not return until the epilogue.",
+    "replace": "The chorus figure appears only for the two prologues and does not remain to watch the action.",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F4: The play has a second Chorus prologue in Act Two and no Chorus epilogue."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 3,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n22\">On playwright&rsquo;s intentions questions about themes, the trap is to describe a theme and then list evidence without explaining what Shakespeare wants the audience to <em>feel</em> or <em>understand</em> at that specific moment. The command word &ldquo;analyse&rdquo; means: identify the technique, explain how it works, state its effect on the audience, and make a judgement about why Shakespeare made that choice. For <em>Romeo and Juliet</em>, examiners note that the best answers hold fate and free will in tension rather than choosing one: the play seems to offer both simultaneously, and that is the point. In design and performer questions about themes, be concrete &mdash; a gobo of star patterns on the stage floor, a colour-coded costume scheme, a performer&rsquo;s movement vocabulary that slows with age. &ldquo;Dark lighting to show the theme of fate&rdquo; is the response that will not progress beyond the developing band.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n22\">For the 12-mark set-text question, focus on the named performer&rsquo;s use of performance space and interaction in the extract. Explain specific choices and their effect on the audience; do not turn the response into a literary essay about theme alone.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F5: AQA's 12-mark set-text task is a performer task, not an analyse-the-playwright's-intentions question."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 4,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n23\">On playwright&rsquo;s intentions questions that ask about context, the most common error is presenting historical information without connecting it to a staging consequence. Every piece of context you cite must earn its place by linking to a specific design or performance choice and an effect on the audience. The Elizabethan shared-light stage, the boy actor convention, the patriarchal household &mdash; none of these is a stand-alone fact; each is a prompt for a staging choice. When you write about context in extended-response questions, use the formula: &ldquo;Because [context], a director might [specific staging decision] to communicate [effect on audience] because [thematic meaning].&rdquo; The examiner is looking for that chain of reasoning, not the historical fact in isolation. Also note that knowing Arthur Brooke&rsquo;s poem as the source is useful only when you use it to explain what Shakespeare changed and why those changes matter for staging.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n23\">For the 4-mark design question, make the specified social, cultural or historical context visible in practical design details for the extract. Name materials, colour, condition, scale or positioning that suit the context, rather than adding a separate history paragraph.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F6: AQA's context-linked set-text task is the 4-mark design question, not a playwright's-intentions question."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 5,
+    "field": "content_html",
+    "find": "<dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"A fourteen-line poem in iambic pentameter, typically structured as an octave and a sestet, with a concluding rhyming couplet.\">sonnet</dfn>",
+    "replace": "<dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"A fourteen-line poem, often written in iambic pentameter; Shakespearean sonnets have three quatrains and a concluding rhyming couplet.\">sonnet</dfn>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F7: The definition conflated a Petrarchan octave-and-sestet form with a Shakespearean final couplet."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 5,
+    "field": "content_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n11\">Shakespeare does not give every character verse. The Nurse, the servants and Peter speak almost entirely in <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"Speech in plays that is written without metrical structure, typically used for lower-status characters or comic exchanges.\">prose</dfn>. This is not random: prose signals lower social status, earthiness and comedy. A performer playing the Nurse can find permission in this: the Nurse does not have to modulate her delivery to a regular beat. She can interrupt herself, trail off, repeat, speed up in excitement, slow down in self-pity &mdash; the prose form invites natural, <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"Depicting life as it is, without idealisation, with authentic speech, behaviour and psychology.\">naturalistic</dfn> vocal behaviour in a way that strict verse does not.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n11\">Shakespeare does not give every character verse. The Nurse uses both <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"Speech in plays that is written without metrical structure, typically used for lower-status characters or comic exchanges.\">prose</dfn> and verse. Prose often suits her comic, conversational exchanges, while verse can sharpen emotional intensity. A performer can use the form as one clue to changes in pace, breath and pressure, rather than treating it as a fixed rule of <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"Depicting life as it is, without idealisation, with authentic speech, behaviour and psychology.\">naturalistic</dfn> delivery.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F8: The Nurse does not speak almost entirely in prose."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 5,
+    "field": "content_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n12\">A crucial teaching moment concerns Act 4: when Juliet is feared dead, the Nurse shifts into verse. For a performer and director, this is a precise signal from Shakespeare &mdash; the intensity of grief elevates even the Nurse beyond her usual register. A lighting designer might underline this shift: the warm, comic amber that has lit the Nurse's scenes could bleed away, replaced by a cooler <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"A broad area of even lighting that covers the whole stage or a large section of it.\">lighting wash</dfn> of pale blue, marking the tonal shift that the change of form announces.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n12\">In Act Four, Scene Five, the Nurse&rsquo;s verse as she finds Juliet can heighten the force of her grief. A lighting designer might underline the emotional shift: the warm, comic amber that has lit the Nurse&rsquo;s scenes could bleed away, replaced by a cooler <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"A broad area of even lighting that covers the whole stage or a large section of it.\">lighting wash</dfn> of pale blue.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F8: The discovery-scene verse is not the Nurse's sole departure from prose."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 5,
+    "field": "conclusion_html",
+    "find": "<li data-narration-id=\"n22\">Prose versus verse signals social status and emotional register &mdash; the Nurse&rsquo;s shift into verse in Act 4 is a performance cue, not just a literary fact, and a good answer translates every formal choice into specific vocal, physical and design decisions.</li>",
+    "replace": "<li data-narration-id=\"n22\">Prose and verse can signal social status and emotional register &mdash; the Nurse uses both forms, and her verse in Act Four can guide specific vocal and physical choices.</li>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F8: The conclusion repeated the false claim of a unique Act Four shift."
+  },
